{"question": "A 19-year-old female college soccer player presents to a sports medicine clinic with right knee pain. One day prior she twisted her right knee and felt a \u201cpop\u201d while chasing after a ball. She has since felt severe throbbing knee pain and noticed a rapid increase in swelling around her knee. She is able to bear weight but feels \u201cunstable\u201d on her right leg. On exam, anterior drawer and Lachman\u2019s tests are positive. The physician informs her that she has likely injured an important structure in her knee. What is the function of the structure that she has most likely injured?", "options": {"A": "Prevent excess posterior translation of the tibia relative to the femur", "B": "Prevent excess anterior translation of the tibia relative to the femur", "C": "Resist excess varus force on the knee", "D": "Resist excess valgus force on the knee", "E": "Provide a cushion between the lateral tibial and femoral condyles"}, "pred_answer": "B", "gold_answer": "B", "question_domains": ["Sports Medicine", "Orthopedics", "Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation", "Emergency Medicine", "Diagnostic Imaging"], "option_domains": ["Orthopedics", "Sports Medicine"], "question_analyses": {"Sports Medicine": "Based on the scenario presented, the 19-year-old female soccer player is likely suffering from an anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) injury. The key indicators supporting this diagnosis include:\n\n1. **Mechanism of Injury**: The patient twisted her knee and felt a \"pop,\" which is a common description associated with ACL injuries, especially in sports involving sudden changes in direction or pivoting. 2. **Symptoms**: The severe throbbing pain, rapid swelling, and feelings of instability in the knee are classic signs of an ACL tear. The swelling is often due to hemarthrosis (bleeding into the joint) that occurs immediately after the injury. 3. **Physical Examination Findings**: Positive anterior drawer and Lachman\u2019s tests are specific tests used to a