{"question": "A 48-year-old man is brought to the emergency department by his son with a fever over the past day. The patient\u2019s son adds that his father has been having some behavioral problems, as well, and that he was complaining about bugs crawling over his skin this morning even though there were no insects. Past medical history is unremarkable. No current medications. The patient was an alcoholic for several years but abruptly quit drinking 5 days ago. The patient\u2019s temperature is 40.0\u00b0C (104.0\u00b0F), pulse is 130/min, blood pressure is 146/88 mm Hg, and respiratory rate is 24/min. On physical examination, he is confused, restless, agitated, and lacks orientation to time, place or person. Which of the following the most appropriate initial course of treatment of this patient?", "options": {"A": "Chlorpromazine", "B": "Clonidine", "C": "Diazepam", "D": "Haloperidol", "E": "Phenytoin"}, "pred_answer": "C", "gold_answer": "C", "question_domains": ["Emergency Medicine", "Psychiatry", "Addiction Medicine", "Infectious Disease", "Neurology"], "option_domains": ["Psychiatry", "Emergency Medicine"], "question_analyses": {"Emergency Medicine": "The clinical scenario presented describes a 48-year-old man who is experiencing fever, confusion, agitation, and hallucinations (specifically, the sensation of bugs crawling on his skin). He has a history of alcohol use and has recently quit drinking abruptly, which raises the suspicion of alcohol withdrawal syndrome, particularly delirium tremens (DTs). ### Interpretation of the Condition:\n1. **Alcohol Withdrawal Syndrome**: The patient has a significant history of alcohol use and has stopped drinking abruptly 5 days ago. Symptoms of alcohol withdrawal can begin within 6-24 hours after the last drink and can escalate to severe manifestations such as delirium tremens, which typically occurs 48-72 hours after the last drink but can occur later. 2. **Delirium Tremens**: The combination of fever, confusion, agitati